        <div class="faith-intro-body">
          <p class="faith-intro-prose hero-excerpt-dropcap">The Athanasian Creed, despite its name, was not written by Athanasius but emerged from the Latin West sometime between the fifth and sixth centuries. It is the longest and most thorough of the ecumenical creeds, repeating its central confession with the precision of a song so it cannot be misheard: the Trinity in Unity, the Unity in Trinity. Its sharp anathemas - “this is the catholic faith, which except a man believe faithfully, he cannot be saved” - were meant to be read aloud in worship.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
    
